# MicroJS8 -- DigiRig USB serial port stable name + hotplug guards
# =====================================================================
#
# v0.0.18 changes:
#   - Renamed from 99-microjs8-digirig.rules to 50- prefix so this
#     rule's ENV directives are set BEFORE 60-gpsd.rules evaluates
#     them. The previous 99- prefix sorted AFTER gpsd's rule, which
#     meant the ID_GPSD_IGNORE flag could be checked before we set
#     it -- creating a race. The 50- prefix removes the race.
#   - Added BRLTTY_IGNORE_DEVICE for completeness (BRLTTY also
#     probes USB-serial devices; this is rare on default Pi OS
#     installs but matters on ClockworkOS and Raspberry Pi OS with
#     accessibility packages installed).
#
# The DigiRig Mobile uses a Silicon Labs CP2102 (or CP2102N) USB-to-UART
# bridge (vendor 10c4:ea60). This is the SAME chip used by many other
# USB serial adapters, so we match by vendor:product but with an extra
# serial-number filter where possible -- DigiRig devices have a
# unique serial number programmed at the factory.
#
# What this rule creates:
#   /dev/digirig    ->  symlink to whichever ttyUSBx the kernel
#                       assigned to the CP2102
#
# Why we need it:
#   The launcher script (microjs8-rigctld-launcher) and the daemon's
#   RtsPttService both expect /dev/digirig to be the canonical
#   stable name. Without this rule, the kernel-assigned ttyUSBx
#   number depends on enumeration order -- could be 0 or 1 depending
#   on whether other USB serial devices came up first.
#
# Group membership:
#   GROUP="dialout" gives the microjs8 user (which is in the dialout
#   group) read/write access without world-writable mode bits.
#
# Adding more DigiRig units:
#   If you have multiple DigiRigs and need to disambiguate, add a
#   second rule line that matches ATTRS{serial}=="<unique-serial>"
#   and creates /dev/digirig1, /dev/digirig2, etc. Then add a new
#   RadioDef in radios.py with preferred_serial_path="/dev/digirig1".
#
# Verifying:
#   sudo udevadm trigger
#   ls -l /dev/digirig
#   udevadm info /dev/ttyUSB0  # see all the ATTRS{} for matching
#
# Defense in depth:
#   The ENV{ID_GPSD_IGNORE}="1" flag is HONORED INCONSISTENTLY by
#   gpsd 3.22+ (Bookworm and newer). The reliable defense is to set
#   USBAUTO="false" in /etc/default/gpsd and pin DEVICES to the
#   actual GPS device's by-id path. See docs/GPSD_COEXIST.md for
#   the operator-facing instructions. The postinst auto-applies
#   this fix on first install where USBAUTO="true" is detected.

# Match: USB serial adapter, Silicon Labs CP2102/CP2102N, on a tty
# interface. The CP2102 enumerates as a single CDC-ACM-like interface;
# no ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM filter needed (vs the QDX which has two).
#
# ENV{ID_GPSD_IGNORE}="1" tells gpsd's hotplug script to leave this
# device alone -- gpsd's default udev rule (60-gpsd.rules in the
# gpsd package) would otherwise grab any USB serial device looking
# for NMEA. On a CP210x this is catastrophic: gpsd opens with RTS
# asserted high, the optoisolator keys the radio's PTT, and the
# CP210x chip latches RTS high until a USB device reset. We don't
# want gpsd touching the DigiRig under any circumstances -- gpsd
# should only ever talk to actual GPS receivers (the u-blox CDC
# ACM on /dev/ttyACM*).
#
# ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1" does the same for ModemManager,
# which also probes USB serial devices for AT command responses.
# Same failure mode as gpsd: probing involves opening the port
# which asserts RTS on CP210x.
#
# ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1" is the per-port variant of the above;
# some ModemManager versions honor one but not the other.
#
# ENV{BRLTTY_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1" tells BRLTTY (the braille terminal
# display driver) to ignore this port. BRLTTY's default udev rule
# probes USB-serial devices looking for refreshable braille displays;
# the same RTS-assertion problem applies. BRLTTY isn't installed by
# default on Raspberry Pi OS Lite but IS on Desktop and on some
# ClockworkOS images.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", \
    SYMLINK+="digirig", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", \
    ENV{ID_GPSD_IGNORE}="1", \
    ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", \
    ENV{ID_MM_PORT_IGNORE}="1", \
    ENV{BRLTTY_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
